 1 big thing: Hill&#x27;s youth wave hits reality 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios Gen Z and millennials launched their biggest bid yet to remake Congress this year. But roughly 7 in 10 were out of contention by early September, Axios' Russell Contreras writes from a new report. 

 Younger generations are struggling to clear the hurdles of a political system that rewards experience, fundraising and established party support. 📈 The report from Future Caucus, a nonpartisan nonprofit that works with younger elected officials, found that a record share of this cycle's congressional candidates came from the country's two youngest political generations.

 Yet only about 21% of Gen Z congressional candidates remained in the race at the report's September cutoff. That figure rose to 30% for millennials, 41% for Gen Xers and 45% for baby boomers. Stunning stat: Incumbents averaged 59 years old — nearly nine years older than challengers.

 🗳️ Yes, but: Young candidates are scoring some wins.

 In Colorado, 29-year-old democratic socialist Melat Kiros ousted 15-term Democratic Rep. Diana DeGette in the primary for a heavily Democratic Denver seat. In Iowa, 29-year-old Republican Joe Mitchell, a former state lawmaker, captured his party's nomination for an open House seat. Go deeper ... Read the report . 

 
 
